20:53:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I'm not going to pretend every delivery driver is a hidden genius, because that's obviously not true—and that's not what I was trying to say. You're right: the person dropping your package in a puddle probably isn't a former rocket scientist. But here's where I think we're talking past each other. The mistake you're describing—putting a package in a puddle—isn't a mistake that requires a high IQ to avoid. Any reasonably functional adult knows puddles are wet and packages shouldn't get wet. So if you're assuming the average delivery person is simply too dull to understand that, you're left with a problem: even people you'd consider "not that sharp" generally know not to place things in visible water. Something else has to be going on. What's happening is that the job conditions create a mental state where the obvious gets overlooked. When you're exhausted, racing a clock, thinking about the next twenty stops, and operating largely on autopilot, your brain can fail to register things that are right in front of you. It's not a lack of intelligence—it's attentional blindness, the same phenomenon that makes a sharp, educated person walk into a room and forget why they're there. The puddle isn't missed because the driver can't comprehend it; it's missed because their cognitive resources are fully consumed by the relentless pressure to keep moving. The reason I keep pushing back on the "they're just not sharp" explanation isn't to be politically correct or to romanticize delivery work. It's because that explanation lets the companies off the hook completely. If the workers are simply dim, then no policy change, no better training, no humane routing algorithm will fix it—you'd just have to hope for smarter workers, which isn't actionable. But if the errors are produced by the system, then the system can be changed. And that's actually hopeful, because it means these mistakes aren't inevitable. You can absolutely still be furious that your package got ruined. That's a legitimate grievance. But pinning it on the raw intelligence of the person doing the job misses the real engine of the problem. A diligent, smart person thrown into the same meat grinder can end up making the same dumb-looking mistakes. That's not an excuse for them—it's an indictment of the conditions they're placed in.
