20:54:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right that the stereotype is widespread—it shows up in jokes, memes, sitcoms, and offhand comments. And when a stereotype is that pervasive, it's natural to assume there must be some truth to it. So let's take that seriously: the pattern of mistakes is real, and people have noticed it long enough for it to become a cultural punchline. The question is what conclusion to draw from it. Stereotypes often form around highly visible, repeated behaviors. Delivery workers interact with the public constantly, and when they make a mistake, it's immediately obvious and personally affects the customer. The same thing happens with traffic cops being "power-trippers," DMV employees being "slow," or fast-food workers being "lazy." These stereotypes don't arise from nothing—they crystallize around frustrating interactions that people share and exaggerate. But what they usually capture is how a job *structures* behavior, not the innate traits of the people in it. Think of it this way: if you took a random sample of the population and put them in a delivery job with the same crushing time pressure, poor training, and algorithm-driven demands, a significant number of them would start making the exact same "dumb" mistakes. Their fundamental intelligence wouldn't change, but their error rate in that specific context would skyrocket. The job is what's producing the stereotype, not the intelligence level of the people who fill it. There's also a class element to why the "dumb delivery guy" stereotype sticks. We tend to intellect-shame low-status workers in ways we don't with higher-status roles that also make errors. Financial analysts have made catastrophic miscalculations that crashed economies, but they're called "overconfident" or "reckless," not "dumb." Surgeons have left instruments inside patients; that's not seen as evidence that surgeons are low-IQ, it's understood as a systemic failure under pressure. We give some professions the grace of complexity and deny it to others. The universality of the joke tells you more about how we process frustration as a society than about the objective intelligence of delivery workers. It's a convenient shortcut: when someone's job is invisible except for a moment of failure, it's easy to slap a dismissive label on them and move on. It's much harder to examine the working conditions that make those failures likely. I'm not saying your frustration isn't genuine or that the mistakes don't matter. They absolutely do. But the existence of a stereotype isn't proof of its own validity—it's proof that people get annoyed and turn that annoyance into shared humor. The real "some truth" behind it is that the job, as currently designed, produces a high volume of visible errors. Where you attribute the cause of those errors—to the workers' brains or to the system—is a choice, and I think the evidence points strongly to the system.
