ence includes carefulness/attention; (2) jobs without filters for these will attract those lacking them; (3) therefore delivery workers are less intelligent. I can grant points (1) and partially (2), but challenge that the missing filter selects for *any* intelligence deficiency beyond the specific situational tendency to be less careful under those conditions. I can also argue that being careful in a delivery context may not be a stable trait but a response to incentives. I'll structure my response: first, acknowledge the insight that carefulness is part of intelligence and that a lack of filter will result in a different distribution of that trait on average. But then I'll nuance it: carefulness isn't a fixed trait; it can be trained and incentivized; the job actively discourages careful behavior through time pressure, so even careful people will appear careless. The absence of a filter for conscientiousness doesn't mean the pool is full of "dumb" people; it means the job doesn't select for that specific trait, and people who have that trait may not be attracted to the job or may get burned out. I can also point out that many "smart" people are absent-minded in everyday tasks, so being careless about package delivery isn't a perfect proxy for general intelligence. But the user may counter that it's a pattern, not a one-off. I'll try to find a middle ground where I admit the distribution is different, but I resist the conclusion that delivery drivers are "not that bright" as a general assessment. I'll try to turn it into a question of trait-context mismatch rather than overall dullness. I'll end with a note that even if we grant the average is lower on some relevant traits, it's still a harmful stereotype to paint all of them that way. But I need to avoid being preachy. The user is being logical, so I'll respect that.
